===Phonotactics===
Each [[syllable]] of Wagiman  contains an [[syllable onset|onset]], a [[syllable nucleus|nucleus]] and an optional [[syllable coda|coda]]. This may be generalised to the syllable template CV(C). The coda may consist of any single consonant, a continuant and a glottal stop, or an approximant and any stop.

At the word level, Wagiman has a [[mora (linguistics)|bimoraic]] minimum, meaning that if a word consists of a single syllable, it must have either a [[long vowel]] or a coda. Examples of monosyllabic words in Wagiman include {{Lang|waq|yow}} {{IPA|[jɒʊ]}} 'yes', or {{Lang|waq|jamh}} {{IPA|[ɟʌmʔ]}} 'eat.{{gcl|PERF}}'.

The retroflex approximant 'r' {{IPA|[ɻ]}} is not permitted word-initially and instead becomes a lateral 'l'. This only affects verb roots, as they are the only part of speech that takes prefixes and are therefore the only possible part of speech for which word-initial and word-medial environmental effects can be observed.

The verb {{Lang|waq|ra-ndi}} 'throw', for instance, surfaces as {{Lang|waq|la-ndi}} when inflected for third-person singular subjects (he/she/it), which are realised by invisible, or [[Null morpheme|null]] morphemes. but as {{Lang|waq|nga-ra-ndi}} when inflected for a first-person singular subject (I). When preceded by a syllable with a coda, the 'r' similarly moves to 'l', as in ''ngan-la-ndi'' 'he/she/it threw you'. In short, the retroflex approximant 'r' {{IPA|[ɻ]}} is only realised as 'r' when it occurs between two vowels. Elsewhere, it becomes a lateral approximant 'l'.

====Heterorganic clusters====
Consonant clusters across syllable boundaries do not assimilate for place in Wagiman as they do in many other languages. This means that a nasal in a syllable coda will not move to the position of the following syllable onset for ease of enunciation. In English and most other [[Indo-European languages]] with the exception of [[Russian language|Russian]], this movement occurs regularly, such that the prefix ''in-'', for example, changes to ''im-'' when it precedes either a ''p'', a ''b'' or an ''m''.
:''in'' + ''possible'' → ''impossible''
:''in'' + ''balance'' → ''imbalance''
:''in'' + ''material'' → ''immaterial''
Wagiman does not do this. A nasal in a coda retains its position regardless of the following consonant:
:{{Lang|waq|manyngardal}} 'tongue' {{IPA|[maɲŋaɖaɫ]}}
:{{Lang|waq|binkan}} 'bream' ''(fish spec.)'' {{IPA|[bɪnɡan]}}
:{{Lang|waq|ngan-bu-ni}} 's/he hit me' {{IPA|[ŋanbʊnɪ]}}
If Wagiman constrained against heterorganic clusters and assimilated them for place, as English does, these words would surface as {{IPA|[ma'''ŋ'''ŋaɖaɫ]}}, {{IPA|[bɪ'''ŋ'''ɡan]}}, and {{IPA|[ŋa'''m'''bʊnɪ]}}.

====Vowel harmony====
High vowels assimilate in height to following mid vowels across syllable boundaries. That is, {{IPA|[ɪ]}} will become {{IPA|[ɛ]}}, and {{IPA|[ʊ]}} will become {{IPA|[ɔ]}}, when the following syllable contains a mid vowel; either {{IPA|[ɛ]}} or {{IPA|[ɔ]}}.

{{interlinear |lang=waq |indent=2
|mi- + -ge → mege
|2SG.IMP {} 'put' {} {'you go and put it'}|}}

{{interlinear |lang=waq |indent=2
|mu- + -yobe → moyobe
|2PL.IMP {} 'stay' {} {'you lot stay'}|}}

Wagiman vowel harmony and other aspects of Wagiman phonotactics require further investigation. It is not known, for instance, whether vowel harmony equally affects unstressed syllables.

==Grammar==
''All grammatical information from Wilson, S. (1999)''<ref name="Wilson, S. 1999"/> ''unless otherwise noted.''

===Parts of speech===
The three most important parts of speech in Wagiman are [[verb]]s, [[coverb]]s and [[nominal (linguistics)|nominals]]. Apart from these, there are a multitude of verbal and nominal [[affix]]es, [[interjection]]s and other particles. [[Pronoun]]s class with nominals.

====Nominals====
Like many Australian languages, Wagiman does not categorically distinguish [[noun]]s from [[adjective]]s. These form one word class that is called nominals. Wagiman nominals take case suffixes (see [[#Nominals 2|below]]) that denote their grammatical or semantic role in the sentence. The grammatical cases are ergative and absolutive, and the semantic cases include instrumental (using), allative (towards), ablative (from), locative (at), comitative (with, having), privative (without, lacking), temporal (at the time of) and semblative (resembling). The dative case can be either grammatical or semantic, depending on the syntactic requirements of the verb.

[[Demonstratives]] are similarly considered nominals in Wagiman, and take the same case suffixes depending on their semantic and syntactic roles; their function within the sentence. That is, the demonstrative {{Lang|waq|mahan}} 'this', or 'here' (root: {{Lang|waq|mayh-}}), may take case just like any other nominal.
*{{Lang|waq|mayh-yi}} this-{{gcl|ERG}} 'this one (did it)'
*{{Lang|waq|mayh-ga}} this-{{gcl|ALL}} 'to here'

=====Examples of nominals=====
*{{Lang|waq|guda}} 'fire', 'wood' {{IPA|[ɡʊda]}}
*{{Lang|waq|wirin}} 'tree', 'stick' {{IPA|[wɪɻɪn]}}
*{{Lang|waq|lagiban}} 'man' {{IPA|[laɡɪban]}}
*{{Lang|waq|gordal}} 'head' {{IPA|[ɡɔɖaɫ]}}
*{{Lang|waq|lagiriny}} 'tail' {{IPA|[laɡɪɻɪɲ]}}
*{{Lang|waq|manyngardal}} 'tongue' {{IPA|[maɲŋaɖaɫ]}}

